What is open, one node each, with the address that shows it.

    lines    ed sends its view every line of the file at each key
             (`ed/7-loop/01-draw.go:/for i := range/`), for the view keeps
             the window. timed in process: 0.2 ms a key on a note of 209
             lines, 1.5 ms on a file of 4011, the nodes half of it; down a
             pipe, to gio, each key is the file again as json. small
             files are the aim, so it stays until a measure says it must
             go; then the view asks for the lines it shows. a scroll still
             writes the screen whole, where a move writes its rows alone
             (`tty/term.go:/Frame shows a screen/`); a wake that changes
             nothing writes the cursor alone, 46 bytes, so no debounce.
             structure, an item lit or a label, is no editor's: something
             beside ed may say it, as a language server would.

    shell    what fm runs for you to read could run in the shell below
             it, typed there as you would type it: v, w and F borrow fm's
             own pane today and pause on it
             (`fm/4-main/13-spawn.go:/p.Run()/`). the nature, by who
             reads what comes out:
               you      v w F, git, a verb on cur: the shell, so it is in
                        the history, scrolls, and runs again from there
               fm       f s m z: a pick fm acts on, so fm runs it
               itself   e o a V: a program with a screen, beside
             the shell is the window's: studio names its slot
             (`studio/5-verbs/06-sendverb.go`), and outside studio it is
             the first pane, which `tmux/conf:/main-vertical/` keeps
             left. what makes it unclean: fm has never typed at the shell,
             on purpose (`README.md:/never moves it or types at it/`); the
             shell stands in a folder of its own, so v would need cur's,
             not the shell's; and a shell busy with a program, or a line
             half typed, takes the keys as theirs. so it is parked until
             those have an answer.

    gio      the looks of fm's kinds and the lens's are copied into gio
             (`gio/model.go:/looks are how the window draws/`), for a
             program down a pipe says none; a looks message would end it.

    preview  the pane beside fm by what fm is on: a text, ed; a folder,
             its README in ed, as now (`ed/5-editor/17-edits.go`), and
             nothing for a folder with none. ed holds a buffer with no
             file already, but a listing in it would be ed showing what
             no one typed: derived, so a lens's
             (`design/todo:/typed at rest/`). the form worth having
             later: a folder edited as text, a line a row and a save its
             renames, as vidir and dired do; moving lines there would be
             the reorder too, as fm's shift-up does. noted, not built.

    html     a page as a tangle tree: shelf.html a folder of chunks,
             tangled to one file as fm and ed are, so a page of 3000
             lines is small files an outline apart; the shelf says so in
             prose (`todo/studio/shelf:/carries its parts by/`). tangle is
             go's today: it takes only .go chunks and gofmts what it
             makes (`tangle/4-run/01-collect.go:/".go"/`), and a folder
             and the file it makes cannot share a name, so the page is
             made elsewhere. noted, not built.

    t        the second noun, deferred by `design/pronoun:/two-noun verbs/`.
             a second noun and not a plural one — that is many, below.

    many     the plural noun is not cur, and it exists twice already:
             fm's sel, the set the space bar fills, and clip, the list
             copy and cut leave — `fm/4-main/19-targets.go` is the
             fallback, selection else cursor. the finders are plural
             too, and fzy is what makes them one, so git add
             `{f '\.go$'} wants no pronoun at all.
             cur stays singular, and not for want of mechanism: a file
             holds lines and both shells splat a capture into argv, so
             git add `{c} would survive three of them. it is that every
             verb would then have to choose first, all or error — e on
             three files, o on three extensions, a on whose directory —
             and this would stop meaning this. the word for a set is
             these, and the selection is already it.
             the seam if it is ever wanted:
             `fm/4-main/13-spawn.go:/m.cur()/` is why the selection
             reaches the tree-writes and not the read verbs. targets()
             is one line there — and then e holds three paths and ed
             opens one, so the verb end owes the same answer.

    drift    the readme's non-goals leave out motion counts;
             `ed/cheatsheet:/leading count/` and normalmode have them.
             `tmux/cheatsheet:/search the pane backward/` describes a
             branch `sh/p` does not have; p ends at the git check. the
             readme's dependency table says it lists what is spawned, and
             omits what o and p spawn: microsoft-edge, zathura, mpv, mocp,
             foliate, xchm. page is the only opener named.

    rule     the rule tree is the thesis, and 48 of its 120 leaves are
             empty. `rule/general/reference:/unwritten, not absent/`
             allows it, and the name is the rule for terse or dry — not
             for pyramid, root, core, cloning, or fourteen of the
             seventeen under learning. the readme sells rule as how the
             work is done; at two fifths it is a table of contents. the
             leaves that work share one shape, a claim then the test, and
             the test line is all most of the empties need. otherwise
             `rule/general/terse:/delete the line/` applied to the tree:
             delete the leaf, and if the meaning survives it was noise.

    dry      one fact, three owners. where cur lives — `sh/c:/$"id/`,
             `sh/m:/$"id/`, and for fm, ed and studio
             `state/state.go:/Path is where a window's state/`; how to
             find the window — `sh/c:/window_id/`, `sh/m:/window_id/` and
             `state/state.go:/window_id/`, three edits for one fix.
             `design/plumbing:/one owner/` is the answer already written.
             fm forks c once at startup, `fm/4-main/24-here.go`; a c -w
             that prints this window's cur path lets fm and m ask instead
             of recompute. fm still writes the file itself, so the fork
             per keystroke stays avoided.

    exec     o runs executables, and a double-tap reaches o.
             `sh/o:/test -x/` execs what passes, and
             `tmux/conf:/double-tap/` makes the touchpad's accident the
             same gesture as right-click: a tap on a word that names ./w
             runs sudo apt-get and rm -rf in a split.
             `design/address:/the sibling verb/` defers exec; it is live
             already, folded into o by accident. a confirm on that branch,
             or keep it off the click path.

    go       the tree is invisible to go's tools. go build ./... and go
             vet ./... choke on the chunks — expected 'package' — and
             gopls with them. go vet main.go per directory works and is
             clean, and nothing says so: a line in the readme and a vet
             step in build keep the trade honest. beside it
             `tangle/5-splice/02-expand.go:/return m/` leaves an
             unresolved <<name>> in the output silently, so the failure
             surfaces as a gofmt warning and then a compile error three
             steps on. check is right there.

    save     ed protects the wrong file: `sh/c:/temp then rename/` for a
             one-line pronoun, `ed/1-buffer/08-save.go:/os.WriteFile/`
             truncating the work in place.

    count    the count replay still reaches keys that prompt
             (`ed/3-mode/06-normalmode/04-key.go:/e.mode.key(e, k)/`):
             5/ asks five times, 5? shows help five times. a rep bit on
             the binding would stop it at a key that prompts; not worth
             the field until it bites.

    ls       `fm/4-main/28-loop.go:/for !m.done/` reads and sorts the
             directory on every key, j included. it is what makes the
             cursor survive a rebuild for free; measure on a large
             directory before touching it.

    resize   `tty/line.go:/t.Size()/` takes the height once, so a resize
             mid-prompt paints the prompt on the old row; the resize key
             falls through default.

    check    `sh/check:/set -- ./` defaults to the whole tree, which drags
             .git and bin through grep and flags every camel in go. its
             targets are rule and todo.

    clash    enter at a clash prompt replaces:
             `fm/3-actions/16-paste.go:/name == ""/` reads the empty
             answer as the default it shows, name [foo]. every other
             prompt here does the same, so it is consistent; it is also
             the one place a default destroys. overwrite as a typed name,
             empty as cancel, would make it an act.

    plumb    ~/lib/plumbing is still a second table: djvu to evince and
             mp3 to mpv, where `sh/o` says zathura and mocp. untested,
             for nothing is running to test it against; an edit rule
             ahead of it if acme should open files itself, and acme's
             button 3 then gets the same chain as right-click. delete the
             file outright if acme never opens — the change is a deletion
             either way (`general/terse`). whether acme opens is unknown.
             the change, from `design/plumbing:/becomes a shim into p/`:
                 type is text
                 data matches '[a-zA-Z0-9_\-./:~@#]+'
                 plumb to open
                 plumb start p $0

    go-term  for the terminal use case, the go terminal library to explore
             beside gio. check it against tty/, the terminal layer this
             repo already has, before adding another.

    term     some of fm and ed works only on a terminal, so swapping the
             view does not carry it. read in the code, not tried by hand.
             the test for each: can every view do it in messages alone?
             help is only keys and words, yet ? gives up
             (`fm/4-main/10-help.go:/needs the terminal/`,
             `ed/3-mode/07-help.go:/needs the terminal/`). start here:
             send it as nodes, wait for a key, likely with no new
             message. a view keeps some keys, t among them, so not any
             key can close it.
             the pickers need a terminal
             (`fm/4-main/22-pick.go:/m.t == nil/`): draw the choices
             through the view, or run the picker beside the window and
             take its answer.
             only o and e run beside a window
             (`fm/4-main/13-spawn.go:/Off the terminal, in a window/`):
